EXT. BRAZOS RIVER FERRY - DUSK

A flat-bottomed ferry the colour of old tobacco hangs on a rope across a river the width of a town street and the colour of weak coffee. Sun lies low and red behind the cottonwoods.

MARSHAL AUGUSTINE PELL, 50s, long grey coat, a face like cracked saddle leather, stands at the bow with his hand on his Colt. Beside him sits LOLA TREVINO, 20s, wrists bound with rope, hair full of dust, staring at the water.

At the stern, BARNABY, 70, the ferryman, thin as a fence rail, hauls on the rope hand over hand. Across the river, on the far bank, two riders have stopped under the trees: the brothers DUNCAN and ROOK STALLWORTH, one fat, one wiry, rifles across their saddles.

PELL
Barnaby. How long to the landing?

BARNABY
Ten minutes. Eight if you quit looking at them.

PELL
They're not going anywhere.

LOLA
Neither am I.

Pell does not turn. Duncan raises a hand. Rook, beside him, does not.

The ferry creaks on. Rope hisses through Barnaby's gloves. The riders begin to walk their horses down to the water.
